le over muggles and  say it was all for the greater good  it was dumbledore's brother aberforth  who became concerned with dumbledore's  ambitions  though there's no evidence he knew that  romantic love was one of the reasons for  his brother's fascination with  grindelwald  aberfurth's suspicions resulted in a  three-way duel between aberfurth  dumbledore and grindelwald  and when ariana tried to interfere she  fell victim to a rogue curse  to whom the curse belonged is still  unknown but grindelwald  immediately fled while it's clear  dumbledore supported grindelwald's  message for a while  what is it exactly that caused him to  change his mind  of course there was the loss of his  sister as well as grindelwald's  willingness to fight dumbledore's  siblings in the first place  the duo likely showed dumbledore that  trust was broken between the two wizards  dumbledore probably viewed the duel as a  betrayal but grindelwald may have seen  dumbledore protecting his  family as a betrayal as well jk rowling  insists that there was a passionate  intimate side to the wizard's  relationship even calling it  incredibly intense but that in writing  she was a lot less concerned with that  and more focused on their emotional  journeys a lot of the emotional turmoil  is caught up in the fact that the two  wizards  once incredibly bonded were no longer  able to support each other's choices  when dumbledore sees grindelwald in the  mirror of aerosaid  it's clear that there's a deep longing  there it shows us our hearts deepest  desires  so there's no doubt that dumbledore  feels an unbreakable bond with the dark  wizard  dumbledore's admission that he and  grindelwald oh we were closer than  brothers  further confirms that grindelwald meant  a lot to him and his lie to harry about  seeing socks in the mirror  also points out that the bond is much  more than he'd like to admit  dumbledore's admission to harry about  seeing himself in grindelwald as young  leaders of the revolution  is definitely a part of the reason he  won't face him he feels he will be  tempted to join him if he lets himself  get too close  i cannot move against grindelwald it has  to be you  on the other hand he could be forced to  eliminate him which would make  dumbledore feel even worse so the blood  pact the two wizards made is not the  only thing keeping them apart  but it is symbolic of all these  emotional complications  the timing of the blood pact is unknown  but it's likely it was made before the  duel that took ariana  if that's the case then dumbledore might  also be running from his own  responsibility in facing grindelwald  again  and the possibility that the rogue curse  that claimed his sister was  actually his fault did he ever mention  my name  did he ever mention hers a blood pact is  a magically binding agreement that  involves two people cutting their hands  with their wands  interlacing them and making an oath two  glowing drops of blood rise from their  palms and become one  a vial forms around the drop enclosing  it and becomes almost impossible to  break  we know that in 1927 grindelwald  possessed the vial from his pact with  dumbledore  though we have no idea how long he had  it for grindelwald held onto it  to protect it from dumbledore attempting  to find a way to destroy it  that is until newt's niffler got its  paws on it the thing about the blood  pact is that while it meant that  dumbledore couldn't go against him  it also meant that grindelwald's hands  were tied the blood pact signifies  a lot of problems for the two wizards so  why didn't they use the unbreakable vow  the vow would have made it impossible  for one to move against the other  but the problem with the vow is that  it's very one-sided  there's an imbalance of power because  one person swears to the other to uphold  their promise or  face unbearable pain dumbledore and  grindelwald might have made a blood pact  because they truly care about each other  since both are equally bound to the oath  it suggests that they saw each other  as equals the two ultimately fight in a  legendary duel in 1945 when dumbledore  gains possession of the elder wand  as for how that became possible in the  crimes of grindelwald dumbledore says  that he may be able to destroy the vial  that binds them  can you destroy it maybe but it all  comes down to one question  were dumbledore and grindelwald really  in love with each other  in 2005 rowling said of dumbledore i see  him as isolated  and a few people have said to me rightly  i think that he is detached  we know eventually he defeats  grindelwald but we don't know how he  breaks the pact  we don't even know the words used in the  oath but from what we know of his  character  and what rowling has said about his  loneliness we know that whatever  dumbledore had with grindelwald  affected his entire life even after  finally defeating him  the teenager received a wound from which  dumbledore never recovered  there was definitely some love on his  side and he never truly forgot about  what he shared with grindelwald  on the other hand what we know of  grindelwald is decidedly  not loving he's incredibly powerful  easily manipulates people  and exploits their vulnerabilities he's  a mastermind in  all senses of the word it's possible  that grindelwald never felt  anything for dumbledore he just wanted  to take advantage of his mind and use  him to find the deathly hallows  at the end of the crimes of grindelwald  he even replaces one dumbledore  with another  still we can't deny that the two wizards  were very similar when they were young  they wanted to reshape the wizarding  world they thirsted for power  and they were both brilliant they  supported and understood each other  for grindelwald that might have been  love and keeping the blood pact could  have had sentimental value  along with practicality if dumbledore  had not changed his mind  perhaps they would have been the most  powerful dark wizards in hi